"""Original myeloid chapter: fourteen AML and APL questions."""
from qbank_authoring import question,ref,finalize
EL=ref('ELN: Diagnosis and Management of AML in Adults, 2022','https://doi.org/10.1182/blood.2022016867')
AP=ref('ELN: Management of Acute Promyelocytic Leukemia','https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6509567/')
OL=ref('ASH: Newly Diagnosed AML in Older Adults Guideline, 2025','https://ashpublications.org/bloodadvances/article/10/6/1897/548455/American-Society-of-Hematology-2025-guidelines-for')
MR=ref('ELN-DAVID: AML Measurable Residual Disease Consensus Update, 2025','https://doi.org/10.1182/blood.2025031480')
QU=ref('FDA: Quizartinib for Newly Diagnosed FLT3-ITD AML','https://www.fda.gov/drugs/drug-approvals-and-databases/fda-approves-quizartinib-newly-diagnosed-acute-myeloid-leukemia')
rows=[]
def q(*a,**k):rows.append(question(*a,**k))
q('A marrow contains blasts with Auer rods and myeloperoxidase positivity. Which lineage is most strongly supported?', 'Myeloid',[
 ('Mature B lymphoid','The described morphology and enzyme are not its defining pattern.'),('Plasma cell','Plasma cells have a different mature morphology and phenotype.'),('Mature T lymphoid','This does not explain MPO-positive blasts with Auer rods.')],
 'Morphology and lineage markers work together', '**Auer rods and MPO support myeloid differentiation.**',['APL is an important urgent subtype when abnormal promyelocytes and coagulopathy are present.','Full flow and genetic assessment define the leukemia more precisely.'], '**Do not stop at the lineage label.**',['Molecular and cytogenetic results affect classification and treatment.','Assess immediate bleeding, infection and leukostasis risks.'],[['Auer rods','Myeloid clue'],['MPO','Myeloid differentiation'],['Next step','Integrated genetic and immunophenotypic diagnosis']],[EL])
q('Which statement about the blast threshold in modern AML classification is correct?', 'Some genetically defined AML entities can be diagnosed below the traditional 20% threshold, with criteria depending on the classification system',[
 ('Every AML diagnosis always requires at least 20% blasts without exceptions','Genetic entities create important exceptions.'),('Any single myeloid mutation proves AML at any blast count','Clonal hematopoiesis and other disorders can carry mutations.'),('The WHO and ICC criteria are identical for every genetic entity','Their thresholds and entity definitions differ.')],
 'Genetics has changed a purely numerical classification', '**The recurrent defining alteration and the chosen system both matter.**',['State WHO or ICC when applying a specific threshold.','A mutation such as DNMT3A alone is not a universal AML-defining event.'], '**Integrate morphology and the clinical setting.**',['Avoid relabeling every clonal cytopenia as AML.','Use specialist hematopathology to resolve borderline cases.'],[['Traditional threshold','20% for many settings'],['Genetic entities','Defined exceptions'],['Application','System- and entity-specific criteria']],[EL])
q('A patient has abnormal promyelocytes, mucosal bleeding, thrombocytopenia and low fibrinogen. APL is strongly suspected. What is the immediate disease-specific action?', 'Begin ATRA promptly while urgently confirming PML::RARA and supporting the coagulopathy',[
 ('Wait for full cytogenetics before giving ATRA','Early hemorrhagic death is a major preventable risk.'),('Perform an elective lumbar puncture before treatment','Invasive procedures can be dangerous during the coagulopathy.'),('Give oral iron and reassess in a month','This is an acute hematological emergency.')],
 'Suspected APL is treated as an emergency', '**Do not delay ATRA for molecular confirmation when the suspicion is compelling.**',['Provide aggressive guideline-based blood-component support.','Confirm the defining rearrangement urgently.'], '**Avoid procedures that add bleeding risk.**',['Follow platelets, fibrinogen and coagulation frequently.','Arrange immediate specialist treatment and monitoring.'],[['Suspicion','APL morphology plus coagulopathy'],['Immediate drug','ATRA'],['Parallel care','Confirmation and hemostatic support']],[AP],style='clinical_scenario')
q('Which rearrangement characterizes classical APL?', 'PML::RARA',[
 ('BCR::ABL1','This is characteristic of CML and selected acute leukemias, not classical APL.'),('RUNX1::RUNX1T1','This defines a different AML subtype.'),('CBFB::MYH11','This is an inv(16)/t(16;16)-associated AML fusion.')],
 'A fusion affecting retinoic-acid signaling creates a differentiation block', '**PML::RARA usually results from t(15;17).**',['Rapid molecular confirmation is central.','Rare alternative rearrangements require careful identification because treatment response can differ.'], '**Use the fusion for disease-specific follow-up.**',['Molecular testing contributes to response and relapse assessment.','The treatment programme is risk-adapted rather than identical to all AML.'],[['Fusion','PML::RARA'],['Typical chromosome event','t(15;17)'],['Clinical importance','Targeted differentiation therapy and monitoring']],[AP])
q('During ATRA/arsenic treatment, an APL patient develops fever, weight gain, pulmonary infiltrates, hypoxemia and hypotension. What complication should be treated immediately while assessing alternatives?', 'Differentiation syndrome',[
 ('Isolated uncomplicated drug fever as the only explanation','The multisystem capillary-leak features require a more urgent assessment.'),('A normal expected response requiring no intervention','The syndrome can be fatal.'),('Iron overload from one treatment dose','This does not fit the acute pattern.')],
 'Differentiation treatment can produce a dangerous inflammatory syndrome', '**Start dexamethasone promptly at clinical suspicion under the APL protocol.**',['Infection and other causes can coexist.','Severe disease may require temporary interruption of differentiating agents.'], '**Support circulation and respiratory function.**',['Review WBC trajectory and cytoreduction needs.','Do not wait for every competing diagnosis to be excluded before treating the suspected syndrome.'],[['Features','Fever, leak and pulmonary dysfunction'],['Immediate treatment','Dexamethasone'],['Severe disease','Specialist support and agent interruption assessment']],[AP],style='clinical_scenario')
q('Which safety assessment is especially important during arsenic trioxide therapy?', 'ECG/QT review with potassium, magnesium and interacting-drug assessment',[
 ('No ECG is needed because arsenic is a differentiation agent','Arrhythmic risk remains relevant.'),('Only ferritin monitoring','This does not address the principal electrophysiological concern.'),('Intentional potassium depletion','Low potassium can increase arrhythmic risk.')],
 'Targeted therapy can have predictable nonmarrow toxicity', '**Arsenic can prolong QT and increase arrhythmia risk.**',['Correct electrolyte abnormalities.','Review other QT-prolonging medicines.'], '**Use the protocol for monitoring and dose interruption.**',['Interpret QT with an appropriate correction method.','Continue surveillance for differentiation syndrome and other toxicities.'],[['ECG','QT assessment'],['Electrolytes','Potassium and magnesium'],['Medicines','Interaction review']],[AP])
q('Why are rapid FLT3 and other actionable mutation results important at AML diagnosis?', 'They can change risk assessment and the choice of targeted components of therapy',[
 ('They replace all morphology and flow testing','The diagnosis remains integrated.'),('Every mutation has the same prognostic and therapeutic meaning','Alterations have different implications.'),('Only mutations found after relapse can guide treatment','Baseline findings can guide initial therapy.')],
 'Genetic testing is time-sensitive clinical information', '**FLT3, NPM1 and other results can alter the management pathway.**',['Cytogenetics remains important.','Risk schemes depend on treatment setting and the complete genetic profile.'], '**Obtain the appropriate panel and urgent assays.**',['Do not infer a target from morphology alone.','Reassess genetics at relapse because clonal composition can change.'],[['Diagnosis','Integrated genetics and morphology'],['Risk','Alteration-specific'],['Treatment','Matched target when evidence supports it']],[EL,OL])
q('Quizartinib added to an intensive treatment programme is specifically approved in the US for newly diagnosed adult AML with which alteration?', 'FLT3 internal tandem duplication',[
 ('Any FLT3 variant regardless of the stated label','The relevant approval specifies FLT3-ITD.'),('PML::RARA','APL follows a different targeted programme.'),('BCR::ABL1 alone','That is not the labeled AML selection biomarker for quizartinib.')],
 'A target family does not make every variant interchangeable', '**The quizartinib programme is FLT3-ITD selected.**',['The approval includes combination induction/consolidation and a maintenance phase after consolidation.','Drug-specific QT and interaction precautions matter.'], '**Choose a FLT3 strategy matched to the disease setting.**',['Other FLT3 inhibitors have different evidence and indications.','Relapsed disease is not automatically treated with the same initial regimen.'],[['Biomarker','FLT3-ITD'],['Setting','Newly diagnosed adult AML with specified programme'],['Safety','Current ECG and interaction guidance']],[QU])
q('An older AML patient is unsuitable for intensive induction but wants active treatment. Which principle reflects modern guidance?', 'Consider an appropriate lower-intensity combination, often a hypomethylating agent plus venetoclax, with mutation-specific alternatives when indicated',[
 ('Age alone means only supportive care can be offered','Fitness, goals and disease biology matter.'),('Every patient must receive full-intensity anthracycline induction','This ignores treatment tolerance.'),('Lower-intensity therapy has no infection or tumor-lysis risk','It can cause substantial cytopenias and metabolic complications.')],
 'Treatment intensity should fit the patient and the disease', '**ASH guidance supports active antileukemic therapy in suitable older adults after shared assessment.**',['Venetoclax-based combinations are important options.','Actionable mutations can support other matched strategies.'], '**Plan monitoring and dose interactions from the start.**',['Azole interactions can substantially change venetoclax exposure.','Assess TLS risk, marrow response and count recovery.'],[['Decision','Fitness, biology and goals'],['Option','Selected lower-intensity combination'],['Safety','Cytopenias, infection, TLS and interactions']],[OL],style='clinical_scenario')
q('What is the therapeutic target of venetoclax in AML?', 'BCL2',[
 ('FLT3','That is a receptor-kinase target of other drugs.'),('CD20','That is targeted by selected B-cell antibodies.'),('PML::RARA','That defines the APL differentiation pathway.')],
 'Apoptotic dependence can be therapeutically exploited', '**Venetoclax inhibits the antiapoptotic protein BCL2.**',['It is used in specific combination regimens.','Sensitivity depends on disease biology and prior treatment.'], '**Manage exposure and toxicity carefully.**',['Strong CYP3A inhibitors require label-based dose modification.','Monitor tumor lysis and prolonged cytopenias.'],[['Target','BCL2'],['Effect','Facilitates apoptosis'],['Practical issues','Interactions and cytopenias']],[EL,OL])
q('A patient with AML has extreme leukocytosis, new hypoxemia and neurological symptoms. What is the best management principle?', 'Urgent assessment for leukostasis with cytoreduction, supportive care and TLS prevention under specialist direction',[
 ('Wait for symptoms to resolve without addressing the leukocyte burden','This can be an emergency.'),('Assume WBC count alone proves leukostasis in every asymptomatic patient','Leukostasis is a clinical syndrome, not a number alone.'),('Give repeated RBC transfusions to a high Hb target before reviewing viscosity','Transfusion decisions require care in this setting.')],
 'Circulating blasts can impair microvascular flow', '**Respiratory and neurological dysfunction are important warning features.**',['Other causes such as infection or hemorrhage must be assessed.','The WBC threshold does not have identical meaning across leukemias.'], '**Treat promptly with a coordinated plan.**',['Hydroxyurea and definitive leukemia therapy are important cytoreductive approaches.','Leukapheresis is selective rather than a universal mortality-proven requirement.'],[['Syndrome','Pulmonary / neurological compromise'],['Action','Urgent cytoreduction assessment'],['Parallel care','TLS and other emergency support']],[EL],style='clinical_scenario')
q('What is the role of measurable residual disease testing after AML therapy?', 'It detects disease below routine morphological resolution and refines relapse-risk assessment in an assay- and timepoint-specific way',[
 ('A negative result guarantees permanent cure','Sensitivity and biological limitations remain.'),('Any persistent age-related DNMT3A clone automatically proves AML relapse','Some mutations can persist as clonal hematopoiesis.'),('MRD replaces all clinical and marrow assessment','It complements those assessments.')],
 'Morphological remission is not always molecular eradication', '**Flow or molecular assays can identify smaller residual populations.**',['The marker, specimen, sensitivity and timing affect meaning.','A suitable diagnostic baseline supports later comparison.'], '**Use results within a validated decision framework.**',['NPM1 and fusion transcripts can be informative markers.','Clonal-hematopoiesis mutations need special interpretation.'],[['Morphology','Lower sensitivity'],['MRD','Assay-defined residual burden'],['Decision','Contextual risk refinement']],[MR])
q('Which AML genetic category is conventionally favorable in the ELN 2022 intensive-treatment risk scheme, absent overriding adverse features?', 'Core-binding-factor AML with RUNX1::RUNX1T1 or CBFB::MYH11',[
 ('TP53-mutated complex-karyotype AML','This is an adverse biological setting.'),('Monosomal-karyotype AML','This is not a favorable category.'),('MECOM-rearranged AML','This is generally adverse in the specified scheme.')],
 'Risk labels belong to a defined classification and treatment setting', '**Core-binding-factor fusions define important favorable-risk entities.**',['Co-mutations and MRD still influence prognosis and decisions.','Favorable does not mean no relapse risk.'], '**Use complete genetics and response information.**',['Do not transfer one intensive-treatment risk model uncritically to every lower-intensity regimen.','Postremission treatment remains necessary.'],[['Favorable example','Core-binding-factor fusion'],['Adverse examples','TP53/complex, monosomal or MECOM settings'],['Refinement','MRD and the complete clinical context']],[EL,MR])
q('What is a central principle for choosing postremission therapy and allogeneic transplantation in AML?', 'Balance relapse risk, MRD, treatment tolerance and transplant-related risk',[
 ('Every morphological remission requires the identical transplant strategy','Disease and patient risks differ.'),('Transplant is never relevant after remission','It can be important in suitable higher-risk disease.'),('A normal peripheral CBC alone proves no consolidation is needed','Peripheral recovery does not establish durable eradication.')],
 'Remission is a treatment milestone rather than the end of planning', '**Postremission therapy aims to prevent relapse.**',['Genetic risk and depth of response help estimate benefit.','Comorbidity and donor factors affect transplant harm.'], '**Reassess the balance with the patient.**',['A favorable-risk MRD-negative case differs from persistent high-risk disease.','Use multidisciplinary transplant input early when appropriate.'],[['Disease risk','Genetics and MRD'],['Patient risk','Fitness and organ function'],['Decision','Relapse prevention versus treatment harm']],[EL,OL,MR])
